message.content:
Based on my analysis of the radiograph (enhanced, zoomed, and local-contrast views of the periapical regions of both arches), here is my assessment.

Working notes: the panoramic (2444×1292) shows a full dentition with a post-and-core crown on an anterior maxillary tooth, amalgam/metal restorations, and maxillary sinuses dipping toward molar roots. I examined each sextant's apical bone at high magnification and local-contrast enhancement; the following periapical radiolucencies were the ones I could support (boxes are fractions of image width/height from the top-left):

```json
{"lesions": [
  {"box": [0.600, 0.630, 0.680, 0.720], "pai": 3},
  {"box": [0.260, 0.620, 0.340, 0.710], "pai": 3},
  {"box": [0.485, 0.345, 0.560, 0.430], "pai": 3}
]}
```

Caveat on confidence: this is a fairly low-contrast panoramic, so apical changes are subtle. The first two boxes mark focal apical rarefaction in the mandibular first-molar regions (mineral loss without a sharply corticated cavity → PAI 3); the third marks the periapical bone of the post-and-core–restored anterior maxillary tooth. I deliberately did **not** include the rounded radiolucencies near the lower premolar apices (likely the mental foramina) or the sinus-related radiolucencies over the upper molar roots, since those are anatomic rather than periapical lesions. If you want, I can render an annotated overlay of these three boxes on the image so you can verify them visually.
